packgen's output is going anywhere commercially significant.


                        PACKGEN OUTPUT EXCEPTION

packgen is licensed under the GNU General Public License, version 3, a
copy of which is in the file LICENSE.

The files packgen generates necessarily contain code written by packgen's
author: the PACKGEN_STATIC_ASSERT macro block, the packgen_* helper
functions, and the body of the marshal and unmarshal routines themselves.
Without the permission below, distributing a program built from those
files could be read as distributing a work based on packgen, and so as
requiring that program to be licensed under the GPL. That is not the
intent.

As a special exception, you may create a larger work that contains part
or all of the code packgen emits into its generated output files, and
convey that larger work under terms of your choice, with no requirement
that the larger work itself be licensed under the GNU General Public
License. This permission applies to the generated files whether or not
you modify them.

This exception does not apply to packgen's own source code. If you
modify packgen and convey the modified version, the GPL applies to it in
full, as usual.

Alternatively, if you modify or redistribute packgen itself, you may at
your option remove this exception from your version, which will cause
packgen and the files your version generates to be licensed under the
GNU General Public License version 3 without this exception.

This exception grants no additional permission over packgen's source
code, and it does not limit the permissions the GPL already grants you.


-- Notes for review, delete before adopting ------------------------------

1. Scope. This is modelled on Bison's exception, which is the closest
   precedent: a GPL tool whose output unavoidably embeds tool-authored
   code. Bison's version carries a carve-out preventing you from using
   the skeleton to build a competing parser generator. I have left that
   out, because packgen's emitted helpers are small and general -- a
   NUL scan and a uint64-to-string -- and a carve-out that narrow buys
   little while giving a legal reviewer something to argue about. Add it
   back if you disagree.

2. Sole authorship. Every commit in the history is authored by David
   Horsley, so you can add this unilaterally. Some commits carry
   Co-Authored-By trailers naming an AI assistant; those are git
   metadata and do not create a second rights holder.

3. Suggested rollout, once you are happy with the wording:
     - rename this file to LICENSE-OUTPUT
     - reference it from the Licence section of README.md
     - add a one-line pointer to the generated banner, so whoever
       receives a generated .c can see its licence status without
       having to go and find packgen first, e.g.
           /* Generated by packgen 1.2.3 from packet.h.
            * Do not edit.
            *
            * Byte order: big endian
            * Licence: see the packgen output exception; this file may
            *          be used under terms of your choice.
            */
   I have not made any of these changes -- the wording is yours to
   settle first.
